"geometry": {"coordinates": [85.30249656589586, 27.7140439234106], "type": "Point"}, "id": "006f66e9-3cd8-470d-a019-1324392b0c8a", "properties": {"nodeId": "e871483e-c2b1-11ea-a1e8-0242ac120007"}, "type": "Feature"}], "type": "FeatureCollection"}, "nodegroup_id": "e871483e-c2b1-11ea-a1e8-0242ac120007", "provisional": false, "tileid": "bc628900-813e-4746-9063-650ff66f8ef3"}], "graph_id": "6215ee02-990d-11ea-9766-0242ac130005", "map_popup": [{"language": "en", "value": "<p>A stone inscription at&nbsp;Luti Ajim\u0101\u1e43,&nbsp;Luti, Kathmandu re the renovation of the temple, a land endowment and a cash donation (NS 902)</p>\n"}], "permissions": {"users_with_no_access": [], "users_without_delete_perm": [], "users_without_edit_perm": [], "users_without_read_perm": []}, "points": [{"nodegroup_id": "e871483e-c2b1-11ea-a1e8-0242ac120007", "point": {"lat": 27.7140439234106, "lon": 85.30249656589586}, "provisional": false}], "provisional_resource": "false", "resourceinstanceid": "20aeabb4-efdb-40bf-8d4e-31d4b22ea1a4", "root_ontology_class": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E34_Inscription"}}, {"_id": "c7d1edc3-a3ae-4dea-a8f7-a1642645fd06", "_ignored": ["tiles.data.2ab25f4a-9323-11e9-8381-0242ac120006.en.value.keyword", "tiles.data.26ac8fa4-af81-11e9-a3d8-0242ac120005.en.value.keyword", "tiles.data.66fd9c70-ce1b-11e9-b993-0242ac140002.en.value.keyword", "tiles.data.fb0a5644-b8f7-11e9-b8a9-0242ac120007.en.value.keyword", "tiles.data.d9f64830-9fc2-11e9-a638-0242ac140004.en.value.keyword", "tiles.data.d9f64efc-9fc2-11e9-a638-0242ac140004.en.value.keyword"], "_index": "danam_resources", "_score": 0.0, "_source": {"displaydescription": "<p>Bhindya\u1e25&nbsp;Hiti is located next to the Bh\u012bmasena Mandira in Bh\u012bmasenasth\u0101na&nbsp;\u1e6cola, Kathmandu. Dissimilar to many other <em>hitis</em> in the Kathmandu Valley, Bhindya\u1e25 Hiti has only one water spout, facing&nbsp;east.</p>\n\n<p>A&nbsp;<a data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://danam.cats.uni-heidelberg.de/glossary/meaning/a77ed470-799e-453b-b914-13860134c4e5/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1615017491222000&amp;usg=AFQjCNHlhrGu9_Yl66XIKDgMyn2hzHg6bQ\" href=\"https://danam.cats.uni-heidelberg.de/glossary/meaning/a77ed470-799e-453b-b914-13860134c4e5/\" target=\"_blank\"><em><strong>makara</strong></em></a>&nbsp;motif is carved on the conduit of the <em>hiti&nbsp;</em>and a statue of Vidhy\u0101dhara and Um\u0101mahe\u015bvara are respectively installed below and above the conduit. Add to this, statues of Bhag\u012bratha, Ga\u1e47e\u015ba, Candra, Lak\u1e63m\u012b, and alike, as well as&nbsp;a&nbsp;<em>\u015bivali</em><em>\u1e45ga</em>&nbsp;are also established in the <em>hiti</em> premises. The Kathmandu Municipality has placed a huge plastic water tank to the northwest of the <em>hiti</em>. The municipality fills it with tankers to distribute water to local residents when the <em>hiti </em>runs dry during the non-monsoon seasons.</p>\n\n<p>There are no evidences&nbsp;to date the initial construction of the <em>hiti</em>. The iconographic program&nbsp;of the statues found in the <em>hiti </em>premises suggest that it could have been built sometime during the Late Malla Period (1482-1768 CE). The stone inscription (from NS 942, i.e. 1822) installed in the <em>hiti</em> premises records the a pavement of the road above the <em>hiti</em> and of the yard in front of the Bh\u012bmasena Mandira.</p>\n\n<p>Bhindya\u1e25 is one of the few&nbsp;<em>hitis</em>&nbsp;still in use. It is a major source of water for the local community during the monsoon. Unfortunately, it merely drips during the non-monsoon seasons. Local residents believe that washing clothes and taking shower in the <em>hiti </em>in the past contributed to its sullied appearance. Thus, a local club, Youth for Nation Nepal, has prohibited to wash clothes and take shower in the present-day.</p>\n\n<p>Water from this <em>hiti </em>is compulsorily used for the daily rituals in the nearby Bh\u012bmsena Mandira. Once in every twelve&nbsp;years, the Bh\u012bmsena statue is shifted to a secret room inside the temple. At the end of this ritual, this statue is ritually cleaned with the&nbsp;water collected from the <em>hiti</em>. The above-mentioned club and the local community periodically clean the <em>hiti </em>premises.</p>\n", "displayname": "Bhindya\u1e25 Hiti || \u092d\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u092f: \u0939\u093f\u0924\u093f ll KAT0930 ", "displayname_language": "en", "geometries": [{"geom": {"features": [{"geometry": {"coordinates": [85.30437320404155, 27.70263573508913], "type": "Point"}, "id": "3813817be1edfd95a03295b9287e7126", "properties": {"nodeId": "d61d682c-b0fe-11e9-8b01-0242ac120006"}, "type": "Feature"}], "type": "FeatureCollection"}, "nodegroup_id": "d61d682c-b0fe-11e9-8b01-0242ac120006", "provisional": false, "tileid": "63c4674f-11b7-4012-8a8d-edf3862f21ff"}], "graph_id": "f35cc1ca-9322-11e9-a5cc-0242ac120006", "map_popup": [{"language": "en", "value": "<p>Bhindya\u1e25&nbsp;Hiti is located next to the Bh\u012bmasena Mandira in Bh\u012bmasenasth\u0101na&nbsp;\u1e6cola, Kathmandu. Dissimilar to many other <em>hitis</em> in the Kathmandu Valley, Bhindya\u1e25 Hiti has only one water spout, facing&nbsp;east.</p>\n\n<p>A&nbsp;<a data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://danam.cats.uni-heidelberg.de/glossary/meaning/a77ed470-799e-453b-b914-13860134c4e5/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1615017491222000&amp;usg=AFQjCNHlhrGu9_Yl66XIKDgMyn2hzHg6bQ\" href=\"https://danam.cats.uni-heidelberg.de/glossary/meaning/a77ed470-799e-453b-b914-13860134c4e5/\" target=\"_blank\"><em><strong>makara</strong></em></a>&nbsp;motif is carved on the conduit of the <em>hiti&nbsp;</em>and a statue of Vidhy\u0101dhara and Um\u0101mahe\u015bvara are respectively installed below and above the conduit. Add to this, statues of Bhag\u012bratha, Ga\u1e47e\u015ba, Candra, Lak\u1e63m\u012b, and alike, as well as&nbsp;a&nbsp;<em>\u015bivali</em><em>\u1e45ga</em>&nbsp;are also established in the <em>hiti</em> premises. The Kathmandu Municipality has placed a huge plastic water tank to the northwest of the <em>hiti</em>. The municipality fills it with tankers to distribute water to local residents when the <em>hiti </em>runs dry during the non-monsoon seasons.</p>\n\n<p>There are no evidences&nbsp;to date the initial construction of the <em>hiti</em>. The iconographic program&nbsp;of the statues found in the <em>hiti </em>premises suggest that it could have been built sometime during the Late Malla Period (1482-1768 CE). The stone inscription (from NS 942, i.e. 1822) installed in the <em>hiti</em> premises records the a pavement of the road above the <em>hiti</em> and of the yard in front of the Bh\u012bmasena Mandira.</p>\n\n<p>Bhindya\u1e25 is one of the few&nbsp;<em>hitis</em>&nbsp;still in use. It is a major source of water for the local community during the monsoon. Unfortunately, it merely drips during the non-monsoon seasons. Local residents believe that washing clothes and taking shower in the <em>hiti </em>in the past contributed to its sullied appearance. Thus, a local club, Youth for Nation Nepal, has prohibited to wash clothes and take shower in the present-day.</p>\n\n<p>Water from this <em>hiti </em>is compulsorily used for the daily rituals in the nearby Bh\u012bmsena Mandira. Once in every twelve&nbsp;years, the Bh\u012bmsena statue is shifted to a secret room inside the temple. At the end of this ritual, this statue is ritually cleaned with the&nbsp;water collected from the <em>hiti</em>. The above-mentioned club and the local community periodically clean the <em>hiti </em>premises.</p>\n"}], "permissions": {"users_with_no_access": [], "users_without_delete_perm": [], "users_without_edit_perm": [], "users_without_read_perm": []}, "points": [{"nodegroup_id": "d61d682c-b0fe-11e9-8b01-0242ac120006", "point": {"lat": 27.70263573508913, "lon": 85.30437320404155}, "provisional": false}], "provisional_resource": "false", "resou